Here is the complete architectural explanation, simple code example, and senior scenario-based breakdown of Spring's Validator Interface based on the official documentation.

📝 Core Architecture Breakdown: Spring's Validator Interface

Spring provides a clean, programmatic way to validate business objects via the org.springframework.validation.Validator interface. Rather than forcing you to tie verification metadata directly to data fields (like JSR-380 annotations do), it separates validation logic into distinct execution components.
The Validator contract relies entirely on two core methods:
1. supports(Class<?> clazz): Asks the validator if it is physically capable of processing instances of the supplied class.
2. validate(Object target, Errors errors): Performs the actual validation logic. If any validation rules are broken, failures are reported back to the Errors container object.

💻 Simple Coding Example

Here is a straightforward implementation demonstrating flat object validation using Spring’s ValidationUtils helper class.

1. The Data Object

java
public class UserProfile {
    private String username;
    private int accountBalance;

    // Standard getters and setters...
    public String getUsername() { return username; }
    public void setUsername(String username) { this.username = username; }
    public int getAccountBalance() { return accountBalance; }
    public void setAccountBalance(int accountBalance) { this.accountBalance = accountBalance; }
}
Use code with caution.

2. The Validator Implementation

java
import org.springframework.validation.Errors;
import org.springframework.validation.Validator;
import org.springframework.validation.ValidationUtils;

public class UserProfileValidator implements Validator {

    // 1. Guard check to ensure this validator only handles UserProfile instances
    @Override
    public boolean supports(Class<?> clazz) {
        return UserProfile.class.equals(clazz);
    }

    // 2. Business validation routine execution
    @Override
    public void validate(Object obj, Errors e) {
        // Automatically rejects if 'username' field is null or empty string
        ValidationUtils.rejectIfEmpty(e, "username", "username.required", "Username cannot be empty.");
        
        UserProfile profile = (UserProfile) obj;
        if (profile.getAccountBalance() < 0) {
            // Log a specific field error code into the Errors registry
            e.rejectValue("accountBalance", "balance.negative", "Account balance cannot be negative.");
        }
    }
}
Use code with caution.

🧠 Senior Scenario-Based Interview Questions & Answers


Scenario 1: Managing Deep Object Composition (The Nested Path Leak)

• Question: You are writing a OrderValidator that contains a nested ShippingAddress object. If you directly instantiate and invoke shippingAddressValidator.validate(order.getAddress(), errors), the error field keys get registered as "city" instead of the nested tree path "address.city". How do you handle nested object pathing correctly using framework utilities?
• Answer: You must use Spring's errors.pushNestedPath(String subPath) and errors.popNestedPath() context blocks, or delegate via ValidationUtils.invokeValidator(...).
• By wrapping the nested validator execution inside a try-finally block that pushes "address", Spring internally prefixes all subsequently registered field errors with the correct nested path string. This ensures that your view layers or JSON error parsers can accurately map the validation failure back to the correct sub-property.
java
try {
    errors.pushNestedPath("address"); // Modifies the validation field scope context
    ValidationUtils.invokeValidator(this.addressValidator, order.getAddress(), errors);
} finally {
    errors.popNestedPath(); // Clean up path stack to avoid leaking context to sibling properties
}
Use code with caution.

Scenario 2: Modern Non-Binding Programmatic Validation (Spring 6.1+)

• Question: In an event-driven system or batch process, you need to execute a quick validation check inside a listener without setting up a heavy Spring Web MVC controller data-binding workflow. How does Spring 6.1+ simplify local standalone validation execution?
• Answer: As of Spring 6.1, you can bypass manual Errors object instantiation entirely by utilizing the new Validator.validateObject(Object) default method.
• This method executes the validation logic locally and immediately returns an immutable Errors abstraction. You can seamlessly chain it with the failOnError(ExceptionFactory) method to immediately transform validation summaries into custom runtime exceptions (such as IllegalArgumentException) in a single fluid line.
java
// Modern Spring 6.1+ inline programmatic validation pattern
myValidator.validateObject(userProfile)
           .failOnError(IllegalArgumentException::new); // Automatically throws if hasErrors()
Use code with caution.
